 "interpretationRules": {
  "encodedRules": [],
  "sourceGuidance": [
   {
    "title": "YSEX-SF Scoring",
    "text": "All 28 items have response options ranging from 1 (None of my sexual experiences) to 5 (All of my sexual experiences). Each of the 14 subscales is scored by summing its two items, giving subscale scores from 2 to 10. Higher scores indicate that a greater proportion of the respondent’s sexual experiences have been motivated by that reason.\nNo total score is computed, and the measure has no norms, percentiles, or clinical cut-offs. The clinical output is the 14-subscale profile. For respondents who have not had sex, the instructions direct them to rate the likelihood that each reason would lead them to have sex, and their profile is read as anticipated rather than experienced motivation.\nSubscale | Items | What it assesses\nStress Reduction | 1, 15 | Sex used to release frustration, anxiety, or stress. Higher scores may indicate that sex serves an emotion-regulation function.\nPleasure | 2, 16 | Sex motivated by physical enjoyment and desire. Typically among the most frequently endorsed motivations.\nPhysical Desirability | 3, 17 | Sex motivated by attraction to the other person’s appearance. The items refer to the person rather than a partner, reflecting that the measure covers casual and non-partnered sex.\nExperience Seeking | 4, 18 | Sex pursued for novelty, adventure, or excitement.\nResources | 5, 19 | Sex exchanged for tangible gain such as money or favours. Elevated scores indicate that exchange-based motivations feature in the respondent’s experiences.\nReproduction | 6, 20 | Sex motivated by the desire to conceive. Scores are strongly context-dependent and interpreted in light of life stage and relationship circumstances.\nSocial Status | 7, 21 | Sex used to enhance reputation or standing.\nRevenge | 8, 22 | Sex used to retaliate against a partner or get even with someone. Elevated scores may indicate unresolved relational conflict.\nPractical | 9, 23 | Sex pursued for practical benefits. The two items span distinct content, one physical and one emotional, so reviewing them individually shows which is driving the score.\nLove and Commitment | 10, 24 | Sex used to express love and feel connected. Low scores in a partnered respondent may be clinically informative about the relational function of sex.\nExpression | 11, 25 | Sex used to mark special occasions or transitions, such as welcomes, farewells, and celebrations.\nSelf-Esteem Boost | 12, 26 | Sex used to feel better about oneself. Elevated scores may indicate that sexual activity is serving a self-worth regulation function.\nDuty/Pressure | 13, 27 | Sex engaged in out of obligation or in response to pressure. Elevated scores can reflect experiences of sexual coercion.\nMate Guarding | 14, 28 | Sex used to keep a partner from straying. Elevated scores may indicate relationship insecurity or fear of abandonment.\n",
    "source_url": null
   },
   {
    "title": "Interpreting the YSEX-SF Profile",
    "text": "The profile is interpreted relatively. The most clinically informative feature is which motivations are most and least frequent for the respondent, rather than the value of any single subscale in isolation. Considered together, the 14 subscale scores describe the overall shape of the respondent’s motivational profile.\nConceptually related subscales are often more informative in combination than any subscale considered alone. Representative examples include:\nFrequent Duty/Pressure alongside frequent Mate Guarding may indicate that sex is functioning to preserve a relationship in which the person feels insecure, directing attention toward relational safety and communication.\nFrequent Stress Reduction alongside frequent Self-Esteem Boost may indicate that sex is serving an emotion-regulation or self-worth function.\nFrequent Pleasure, Love and Commitment, and Expression together describe sexual activity that is intrinsically and relationally motivated.\nInfrequent Pleasure or Love and Commitment in a partnered respondent presenting with sexual dissatisfaction may itself be the clinically salient finding.\n",
    "source_url": null
   },
   {
    "title": "YSEX-SF Norms",
    "text": "NovoPsych does not calculate YSEX-SF percentiles. The only available normative data come from the validation sample of 223 United States adults recruited online, reported separately by gender, with pronounced floor effects on several subscales because most respondents endorse None of my sexual experiences for motivations such as Resources, Revenge, and Reproduction. Percentiles derived from that sample would imply a reference comparison and a degree of precision the data do not support. The measure also has no clinical cut-offs anywhere in the literature. Interpretation therefore rests on the frequency descriptors and the shape of the profile.\n",
    "source_url": null
   },
   {
    "title": "Should clinicians focus on the highest-scoring subscale?",
    "text": "Not necessarily. The pattern across subscales is often more informative than any single score. Most people report a combination of motivations rather than a single dominant reason, and results are best interpreted alongside the client’s history, presenting concerns, and broader clinical assessment.\n",
    "source_url": null
   }
  ],
  "reportScoringGuidance": [
   {
    "documentSha256": "258640f1773d6aca1e71aad9daf3c86390590cced266152e6c8e55814da1e64e",
    "pageNumber": 3,
    "text": "Each of the 14 subscales is scored by summing its two items (response options 1 'None of my\nsexual experiences' to 5 'All of my sexual experiences'), giving subscale scores from 2 to 10.\nHigher scores indicate that a greater proportion of the respondent's sexual experiences have\nbeen motivated by that reason. No total score is computed and no norms, percentiles, or clinical\ncut-offs exist; the clinical output is the 14-subscale profile.\nEach subscale score is accompanied by a frequency descriptor derived from the response\noptions (score 2 = 'None of my sexual experiences'; 3-4 = 'A few'; 5-6 = 'Some'; 7-8 = 'Many';\n9-10 = 'All of my sexual experiences'). These descriptors are frequency labels, not severity\nratings: the clinical relevance of a given frequency depends on the motivation being assessed\nand the respondent's circumstances.\nThe profile is interpreted relatively: the most clinically informative feature is which motivations\nare most and least frequent for the respondent, rather than the value of any single subscale in\nisolation. If either Duty/Pressure item ('I was pressured into doing it', 'I felt obligated to') is\nendorsed above 'None of my sexual experiences', the report displays a note for the clinician, as\nmotivations of this kind range from a sense of relational duty through to unwanted or coerced\nexperiences.\nFor respondents who have not had sex, the instructions direct them to rate the likelihood that\neach reason would lead them to have sex, and their profile is read as anticipated rather than\nexperienced motivation.",
    "usage": "source_guidance_for_review_not_executable_scoring",
    "runtimeEnabled": false
   }
  ],
